\begin{note}
\lstinline{isinstance(obj, cls)} is \lstinline{True} when \lstinline{obj} is an instance of \lstinline{cls}
\emph{or of any subclass of} \lstinline{cls}.
The test is one-directional:
an instance of a child is an instance of its parent, but an instance of the parent is not an instance of the child.
Pass a tuple to test against several classes at once: \lstinline{isinstance(x, (A, B))}.
Prefer \lstinline{isinstance} to \lstinline{type(x) is A},
which is \lstinline{False} for subclass instances and hides builtin subtyping such as \lstinline{bool} being a subclass of \lstinline{int}.
\lstinline{issubclass} performs the same test on classes instead of instances.
This is how \lstinline{except} matches exceptions:
because \lstinline{KeyError} is a subclass of \lstinline{LookupError},
an \lstinline{except LookupError} clause catches it.
\end{note}
